	<description xmlns="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">&lt;b&gt;The essential oral history of hip-hop, from its origins on the playgrounds of the Bronx to its reign as the most powerful force in pop culture&amp;mdash;from the award-winning journalist behind &lt;i&gt;All the Pieces Matter,&lt;/i&gt; the &lt;i&gt;New York Times&lt;/i&gt; bestselling oral history of &lt;i&gt;The Wire&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;ldquo;&lt;i&gt;The Come Up&lt;/i&gt; is Abrams at his sharpest, at his most observant, at his most insightful.&amp;rdquo;&amp;mdash;Shea Serrano, #1 &lt;i&gt;New York Times&lt;/i&gt; bestselling author of &lt;i&gt;Hip-Hop (And Other Things)&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;br&gt;The music that would come to be known as hip-hop was born at a party in the Bronx in the summer of 1973. Now, fifty years later, it&amp;rsquo;s the most popular music genre in America. Just as jazz did in the first half of the twentieth century, hip-hop and its groundbreaking DJs and artists&amp;mdash;nearly all of them people of color from some of America&amp;rsquo;s most overlooked communities&amp;mdash;pushed the boundaries of music to new frontiers, while...</description>
</metadata>
